Good plan — I can help you organize what to ask and what to have checked at your annual physical so you and Dr. Lee can make an informed decision about atrial fibrillation (AF) management and whether a cardiology referral is needed.

First, a few quick clarifying questions (so I can tailor the suggestions):
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation, or is this a new concern (palpitations, irregular pulse, lightheadedness)?  
- Do you have diabetes (I ask because you’re on metformin)? Any history of hypertension, heart failure, prior stroke/TIA, or vascular disease?  
- Any current use of NSAIDs, anticoagulants, antiplatelet drugs, or prescription heart/rhythm meds?  
- Do you have kidney disease (most recent creatinine/eGFR)?  
- Any recent falls or major bleeding history?

If you prefer, I can skip the questions and give general guidance based on common scenarios.

What to review and ask at your visit
- Confirm whether AF is present and what type: paroxysmal (intermittent), persistent, or permanent. If AF hasn’t been documented, ask for an ECG at the visit or ambulatory monitoring (Holter/event monitor) if symptoms are intermittent.
- Stroke risk assessment: ask Dr. Lee to calculate your CHA2DS2‑VASc score and explain what it means for you. (Important factors: age, sex, diabetes, hypertension, CHF, prior stroke/TIA, vascular disease.) If you have diabetes + female sex and no other risks, your score would be about 2 — usually meaning anticoagulation is recommended; confirm all factors with your PCP.
- Bleeding risk and safety: ask for a HAS‑BLED assessment (hypertension, renal/liver function, stroke, bleeding history, labile INR, age >65, drugs/alcohol) and how it affects choice of anticoagulant.
- Anticoagulation options and interactions: ask whether a direct oral anticoagulant (DOAC) or warfarin is better for you given your kidney function, osteopenia, GERD meds (PPIs sometimes used for GERD — discuss interactions), and fall/back pain (NSAID use increases bleeding risk).
- Tests to order now: ECG, basic labs (BMP/creatinine, CBC, LFTs), thyroid function (TSH), A1c/lipids if diabetes, echocardiogram if structural heart disease suspected, and ambulatory monitoring if AF is suspected but not documented.
- Rhythm vs rate control: ask whether symptom control is best done with rate-control meds (beta blockers, diltiazem) or rhythm-control strategies (antiarrhythmics, cardioversion, ablation) based on your symptoms and exam.
- When to refer to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology): ask whether a referral is recommended now. Reasons to refer include:
  - New diagnosis of AF (many PCPs refer for initial evaluation)
  - Recurrent or symptomatic AF despite initial therapy
  - Complex anticoagulation decisions (valvular AF, severe CKD, prior bleeding)
  - Consideration of catheter ablation or other interventional therapy
  - Structural heart disease, syncope, or embolic events despite treatment
If none of the above apply and AF is straightforward (documented, low complexity), some PCPs manage anticoagulation and rate control themselves. But a referral is reasonable if you want a cardiology opinion given your age and diabetes.

Other important points to bring up
- Kidney function: DOAC dosing depends on eGFR — confirm labs.
- Falls and bone health: mention osteopenia and calcium/vitamin D — ask whether anticoagulation has implications for bone health or fracture risk and how to reduce bleeding risk if you have back pain or fall risk.
- Pain meds: tell Dr. Lee about any NSAID or opioid use — NSAIDs increase bleeding risk with anticoagulants.
- GERD meds: if you’re on a PPI, mention it (can be relevant to GI bleeding risk and med interactions).
- Lifestyle and comorbidity optimization: discuss blood pressure control, weight, alcohol intake, and screening/treatment for sleep apnea (OSA increases AF risk).
- Red flags: ask what symptoms should prompt urgent care (chest pain, sudden weakness, severe shortness of breath, fainting).

Suggested phrasing you can use at the appointment
- “I’d like to review atrial fibrillation risk and stroke prevention today. Could you calculate my CHA2DS2‑VASc and HAS‑BLED scores and explain whether I should start an anticoagulant?”
- “Do you recommend cardiology referral (Colorado Cardiology) now or only if AF is confirmed/if I have symptoms? If so, can you place the referral?”
- “Could you order an ECG today and labs (BMP/creatinine, CBC, LFTs, TSH)? If AF is intermittent, please consider a Holter or event monitor.”
- “I have osteopenia and back pain and take calcium/vitamin D and sometimes NSAIDs — how would anticoagulation affect bleeding or bone health, and are any medication interactions I should worry about?”

When referral to cardiology is more strongly indicated
- New, documented AF (most PCPs refer for initial cardiology input)  
- Symptoms not controlled by initial therapy (palpitations, syncope, disabling symptoms)  
- Need for ablation or specialized procedures  
- Complicated anticoagulation decisions (valvular disease, severe CKD, prior major bleeding or thromboembolism)

Safety note
- If you ever experience chest pain, sudden weakness/slurred speech, fainting, or severe shortness of breath, seek emergency care.

Would you like help with any of the following next steps?
- I can draft a short message/email to Dr. Lee requesting these tests and a discussion about AF and cardiology referral.  
- I can draft a one‑page checklist you can bring to the appointment.  
- Or, if you prefer, give your answers to my short clarifying questions and I’ll tailor the checklist and suggested script specifically for you.

